% Heating Pads and Heat Lamps: LCD L33784 and its Policy Article A52502 (DME
% MACs). A policy program of the DMEPOS model: it includes dmepos.le
% (orders, delivery, benefit category, continued need, refills) and states
% when a claim for a heating pad is REASONABLE AND NECESSARY. Only the
% standard electric heating pad (E0210) has coverage criteria: it "is covered
% to relieve certain types of pain, decrease joint and soft tissue stiffness,
% relax muscles, or reduce inflammation", and "is not reasonable and
% necessary to treat pain due to peripheral neuropathy, including but not
% limited to diabetic neuropathy". Everything else the LCD names is denied as
% not reasonable and necessary — the moist electric heating pad (E0215) and
% the water circulating heat pad with pump (E0217), "not established" to be
% reasonable and necessary compared to the standard pad; the replacement
% pump (E0236) and pad (E0249) of a water circulating system; the heat lamps
% (E0200, E0205), whose safety and effectiveness in the home is not
% established — so no rule concludes them. The hydrocollator units (E0225,
% E0239) are "institutional equipment", statutorily noncovered (the Policy
% Article): in scope, with no benefit category.
%
% How the criteria are read. Whether the pad is for one of the purposes the
% LCD covers — certain types of pain, joint and soft tissue stiffness, muscle
% relaxation, inflammation — is one clinical judgment of the treating
% practitioner, accepted or rejected on review: the judged template of
% dmepos.le, "the finding F about B is established" (or unsupported), with
% who found it and why. The finding this policy reads, and the criterion it
% stands for:
%   heating pad indicated (E0210: to relieve certain types of pain, decrease
%   joint and soft tissue stiffness, relax muscles, or reduce inflammation —
%   ONE finding with the purposes as its reasons).
% That the pad is provided to treat pain due to peripheral neuropathy is what
% the record states — a scenario fact — and excludes coverage.
%
% Not modelled: the coding definitions of E0210, E0215 and E0217 (fabric
% cover, automatic shut-off, NRTL certification) and the denial of pads
% billed as E1399 that do not meet them (coding); the short-term replacement
% pad billed as A9999; the nonelectric heating pad or wrap (A9273), which
% the Policy Article denies as not durable medical equipment — the code is
% the cold therapy policy's (cold_therapy.le), which names it too.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    % the record
    *an item* is provided to treat pain due to peripheral neuropathy; undefined.
    % the knowledge base
    heating code *a code* is statutorily noncovered as institutional equipment.
    % derived
    *an item* is a standard electric heating pad.

the knowledge base heating_pads includes these resources:
    dmepos.

the knowledge base heating_pads includes:

LCD L33784 is published at "https://www.cms.gov/medicare-coverage-database/view/lcd.aspx?lcdid=33784".
the text of LCD L33784 is at "sources/lcd/L33784.txt".
Policy Article A52502 is published at "https://www.cms.gov/medicare-coverage-database/view/article.aspx?articleid=52502".
the text of Policy Article A52502 is at "sources/article/A52502.txt".

% What a standard electric heating pad is (the Policy Article's coding
% guidelines).
rule heating_pads_standard_pad with provenance Policy Article A52502,
        confer "A standard electric heating pad (E0210) is a flexible device containing electric resistive elements producing heat":
an item is a standard electric heating pad
    if the HCPCS code of the item is "E0210".

% The hydrocollator units the Policy Article places outside the benefit.
heating code "E0225" is statutorily noncovered as institutional equipment,
    as stated in Policy Article A52502, confer "Hydrocollator units (E0225, E0239) are considered institutional equipment and will be denied as statutorily noncovered".
heating code "E0239" is statutorily noncovered as institutional equipment,
    as stated in Policy Article A52502, confer "Hydrocollator units (E0225, E0239) are considered institutional equipment and will be denied as statutorily noncovered".

section applicability is:

heating_pads has no specific documentation requirements.

% The codes this policy decides: the heating pads, the replacement pump and
% pad, the heat lamps, the hydrocollator units.
rule heating_pads_codes with provenance Policy Article A52502,
        confer "Heating pads are covered under the Durable Medical Equipment benefit":
the policy for code a code is heating_pads
    if the code is in ["E0200", "E0205", "E0210", "E0215", "E0217", "E0225", "E0236", "E0239", "E0249"].

% Their benefit: durable medical equipment, except the hydrocollator units.
rule heating_pads_benefit with provenance Policy Article A52502,
        confer "Heating pads are covered under the Durable Medical Equipment benefit":
the benefit category of code a code is durable medical equipment
    if the policy for code the code is heating_pads
    and it is not the case that
        heating code the code is statutorily noncovered as institutional equipment.

section question is:

% The standard electric heating pad: for one of the covered purposes (the
% finding), and not to treat pain due to peripheral neuropathy.
rule heating_pads_standard with provenance LCD L33784,
        confer "A standard electric heating pad (E0210) is covered to relieve certain types of pain, decrease joint and soft tissue stiffness, relax muscles, or reduce inflammation":
a claim is reasonable and necessary
    if the claim is for an item
    and the item is a standard electric heating pad
    and the item is furnished to a beneficiary
    and the finding heating pad indicated about the beneficiary is established
    and it is not the case that
        the item is provided to treat pain due to peripheral neuropathy.

% No rule concludes the others: "if code E0215 or E0217 is provided it will
% be denied as not reasonable and necessary"; "a replacement pump (E0236) or
% pad (E0249, A9999) will be denied as not reasonable and necessary"; the
% heat lamps (E0200, E0205): "Claims for these items will be denied as not
% reasonable and necessary."
